OES software-attribute admission in a disposable VM
=================================================

Build current liboes/libservice dependencies and the matching libsys first,
then run from /usr/src:

  /usr/local/bin/python3.12 tools/test/oes-admission/build.py

This compiles daemon objects with all logical CPUs and links private static
Capsule/SwitchBoard copies under /usr/obj/usr/src/.../tools/test/oes-admission.
It does not install programs, load host modules, or run either host daemon.
Use tools/test/daemon-runtime/run-vm.py with --oes-admission --cpus 4 and the
usual explicit kernel/root/object/QEMU inputs. The runner keeps its kernel
identity checks, 1 GiB memory, networkless snapshot disk, and mandatory tests.
Each work directory must be new. The provider/client/coordinator are VM-only.

The integration runs real PID1 Capsule and SwitchBoard with staged test bundles.
The managed-unit policies grant system.oes only to OESAllowed/client (UID65534).
OESDenied/client has no attribute; OESWrongAttribute/client has system.OES;
OESRootDenied/client runs as UID0 without the attribute. Every client calls
actual oes_client_create_brokered(). The allowed client queries NOTIFY/global
mode and subscribes through liboes; denial must be an admission-shaped discovery
error, not a transport error. Result files verify expected contents and owner.
Clients wait up to 60 seconds for the provider to receive SwitchBoard's
readiness acknowledgement before beginning their single brokered request. This
fixture barrier does not alter the public API budget or retry a failed request.
Denied clients also wait for the authorized result so an unavailable provider
cannot masquerade as policy denial. Acceptance requires all managed and
application outcomes and clean guest poweroff through Capsule.

OESProvider is an OES-only test provider. It uses the real libservice provider
API and BSDFilesystem oes_factory.c/oes_session.c, but no ZFS storage backend.
The endpoint has requires=["system.oes"] in its own Unit.ucl. This exercises
unit parsing, daemon registration/launch, software identity, discovery policy,
restricted descriptor delivery and public client APIs as one running stack.
It does not qualify the installed BSDFilesystem storage daemon, its ZFS setup,
installer, quotas, or hardware. Existing shipped-manifest and factory tests
remain complementary requirements. A failure or skip is not acceptance.

The runtime PID1 first runs the mandatory and OES native tests. Only after
those pass does it mount private tmpfs runtime/log directories and exec
Capsule, preserving PID1 and the kernel boot-authority
issuer boundary. The loader enables the plane only for this explicit option.
The staged passwd/group databases include the standard capability service
identity as well as the two test identities. No UID-based authority bypass is
added. The coordinator also forks ordinary UID65534 applications: an approved
activation.exec program, an unapproved registered program, and an unregistered
copy of the same executable. Children clear supplementary groups before exec.
A root coordinator checks all seven result files for 25 seconds, prints
/var/log/capability.log and requests guest poweroff through the public
system.lifecycle API. Only that coordinator receives system.switchboard.admin
in its Unit.ucl; UID0 alone is insufficient. The VM
runner requires the baseline pass, admission pass, and clean guest shutdown;
use --timeout 360 for the combined boot. If the coordinator cannot start or
poweroff fails, the VM deadline produces a failure. Do not relax the separate
mandatory 10ms/75ms RPC deadline to obtain a pass.

Coalition lifecycle acceptance
------------------------------
The combined fixture also mounts one disposable tmpfs bundle, CoalitionLifecycle,
and prepares it before handing PID1 to Capsule. Its worker creates a runtime
file with O_EXCL and forks a descendant that retains the descriptor and ignores
SIGTERM. The coordinator updates the worker manifest and requests reload through
system.switchboard. The replacement must start with a clean runtime directory
and both previous processes gone. It then removes the worker from the bundle's
unit list and deletes its unit directory, requests reload again, and requires the old processes, runtime directory,
and service status entry to disappear. A non-activated keeper leaves a valid
bundle so this tests intentional removal, not retention after invalid metadata.
COALITION_LIFECYCLE_PASS is required in addition to the OES/native pass markers.
The fixture does not inject delayed kernel resource callbacks; native cleanup
observer tests cover retained runtime and late completion separately.

Isolated fixture builds
-----------------------
Both build.py and the runtime runner accept --fixture-objtop for a private static
test tree under /usr/obj/usr/src. Kernel/module inputs still come from --objtop.
Capsule and SwitchBoard fixture objects always use private subdirectories rather
than their normal world-build object directories. Build every selected test
against the matching source libraries and libsys; record and check library hashes
across a build if another build is running. An isolated build does not establish
that the shared world/kernel build or package publication has completed.
